                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\phi
                    \&amp; =
                    \\phi \circ id_{X_1 \times X_2}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;=
                    \\phi \circ ( (id_{X_1},0) \circ p_1 + (0,id_{X_2})\circ p_2 )
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\phi \circ (id_{X_1}, 0) \circ p_1 + \phi \circ (0, id_{X_2}) \circ p_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    f_1 \circ p_1 + f_2 \circ p_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\phi' \circ (id_{X_1}, 0) \circ p_1 + \phi' \circ (0, id_{X_2}) \circ p_2
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\phi' \circ ( (id_{X_1},0) \circ p_1 + (0,id_{X_2})\circ p_2 )
                    \\\
                    \&amp;=
                    \\phi' \circ id_{X_1 \times X_2}
                    \\\
                    \&amp;=
                    \\phi'
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1\times X_2
              satisfies the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
              of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            %p
              By a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} dual
              argument, the binary coproduct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊔
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \sqcup X_2
              is seen to also satisfy the universal property of the binary product. By
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Such products which are also coproducts as in prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductsAreBiproducts"}
              are sometimes called
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
              or
              = succeed ";" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums"} direct sums
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+limit"} absolute limits
              for
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enrichment
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              The coincidence of products with biproducts in prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductsAreBiproducts"}
              does
              %em not
              extend to infinite products and coproducts.) In fact, an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete
              just when it is additive and moreover its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotents"} idempotents
              split.
          %p Conversely:
          #SemiadditiveCategory.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              that has all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
              which, moreover, are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
              in that they coincide with finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              as in def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductsAreBiproducts"}
          #SemiAdditivityInducesAbelianMonoidEnrichment.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              In a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SemiadditiveCategory"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
              acquire the structure of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoids"} commutative monoids
              by defining the sum of two morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g \;\colon\; X \longrightarrow Y
              to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
